---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import race from '../content/race.json';
import privacy from '../content/privacy.json';
import { orTbc } from '@src/shared/privacy';

// The club's own privacy notice — the site-wide one, in the club's brand, with no event
// theme anywhere near it.
//
// ## Why there are two notices rather than one
//
// [ADR-011](../../../../docs/architecture/decisions/adr-011-a-race-and-its-runnings.md)
// predicted this page in as many words — *"the right home is eventually `/privacy/`, once
// the club has a second form to cover"* — and accounts are that second form. They are also a
// different **kind** of processing: an entry is what the club holds about somebody for one
// race that happens once, and an account is a standing record of a named person held for as
// long as they have one.
//
// **`/nn/privacy/` stays, and keeps everything that is specific to the race** — the medical
// note, the emergency contact, the payment, and the one-month retention that
// `packages/db/tests/entries-retention.test.ts` ties to `entries.events.medical_retention`.
// Folding that into a general document would not remove the coupling, only make it harder to
// see; the test that stops the club publishing one period and enforcing another is worth more
// than a single file. So the two link to each other, and this one says plainly what it does
// not cover.
//
// ## This page is written from the schema, not from a form
//
// The same rule `/nn/privacy/` states and for the same reason: *under-listing what a
// controller processes is a defect in a notice*. Section 3 below is what
// `20260824090000_create_identity_schema.sql` and Supabase Auth's own tables actually hold —
// which is a good deal more than the three boxes on the sign-up form, because the timestamps,
// the sign-in records, the role grants and the audit trail are all recorded about a person
// and none of them is typed by one.
//
// ## The values that are undecided
//
// Three of them render "To be confirmed by the club" through the shared `orTbc` — the contact
// for data questions, which is `race.json`'s and is the same open decision on both notices;
// how long an account is kept; and whether deleting an account also deletes a race entry by
// the same person. `privacy.spec.ts` counts the marker — three, exactly — so filling one in
// fails that test until it is updated in the same commit, and a settled fact quietly
// reverting to the marker fails it too.
//
// **The settled facts come from `race.json`'s `privacy` key rather than being retyped here**,
// so the two notices cannot disagree about who the controller is or where it is registered.
//
// **A wrong answer on this page is a legal claim, not a typo.** See
// docs/architecture/principles.md#stop-and-ask

const canonical = 'https://new.southvillerunningclub.co.uk/privacy/';

const club = race.privacy;
---

<Base
  title="Privacy notice — Southville Running Club"
  description="What Southville Running Club collects when you have an account on this website, why, who else sees it, how long it is kept, and what you can ask for."
  canonical={canonical}
>
  <h1>Privacy notice</h1>

  {
    /* **`{' '}` is a real space, and it is here because a line break is not.** A newline
    either side of an expression is collapsed away rather than kept as a space, so a sentence
    reads "…company numberending 7549" without it — and Prettier is what moves a bare
    expression onto a line of its own, so a space typed in the source does not survive the
    next format. `privacy.spec.ts` asserts the joined strings as whole strings. */
  }
  <p class="lede">
    This is Southville Running Club's privacy notice. It covers your account on this
    website, and everything else the club does with your details that is not about one
    race.
  </p>

  <div class="privacy">
    <h2>1. Who we are, and how to complain</h2>

    <p>
      {orTbc(club.controller)} is the data controller for the information you give us.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Registered office:</strong>{' '}
        {orTbc(club.registeredOffice)}
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Registered in England and Wales</strong>, company number{' '}
        {orTbc(club.companyNumber)}
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Contact about your data:</strong>{' '}
        {orTbc(club.contact)}
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      If you are unhappy with how we have handled your information, please tell us first —
      we would rather put it right. You also have the right to complain to the Information
      Commissioner's Office at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/">ico.org.uk</a>, or on 0303
      123 1113.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What this notice covers, and what it does not</h2>

    <p>
      It covers <strong>an account on this website</strong>: creating one, signing in, the
      details your account holds, and what the club may let you do once you are signed in.
    </p>

    <p>
      {
        /* The link down. `/nn/privacy/` is the notice with the medical note, the emergency
        contact, the payment and the retention period the database enforces — none of which
        belongs in a general document. */
      }
      <strong>It does not cover entering a race.</strong> What the club collects when you enter{
        ' '
      }
      {race.name}, or ask to hear when its entries open — including anything you tell us
      in the medical box, your emergency contact and your payment — is set out in{' '}
      <a href="/nn/privacy/">the {race.name} privacy notice</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>An account and a race entry are separate things.</strong> Entering a race does
      not create an account, having an account does not enter you into anything, and the details
      on one are not copied to the other.
    </p>

    <h2>3. What your account holds</h2>

    <p>
      This is what the club's records actually contain, rather than only what the sign-up
      form asks for — the times, the sign-in records and the roles below are all held
      about you, and none of them is something you type.
    </p>

    <h3>What you give us</h3>

    <dl class="privacy-pairs">
      <dt>Your email address</dt>
      <dd>
        It is how you sign in, and where we send the emails your account needs —
        confirming your address, and resetting your password if you ask.
      </dd>

      <dt>Your password</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>We never hold your password itself.</strong> It is stored scrambled, in a form
        that cannot be turned back into what you typed, which is why nobody at the club can
        tell you what your password is — only help you set a new one.
      </dd>

      <dt>Your name</dt>
      <dd>So the club can address you as a person rather than as an email address.</dd>

      {
        /* **This is the one place the platform's data-minimisation rule is knowingly
        reversed**, and it is reversed for members rather than for entrants: principles.md
        says a date of birth becomes an age and an address does not persist at all, which
        was written for somebody the club holds data about for one race. `entries` is
        untouched by any of this and still stores neither. #61 is what built the page that
        fills these in — `/account/details/` — and removed the clause that used to say
        there was not yet one. */
      }
      <dt>Your gender, date of birth and address</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Optional, every one of them, and empty unless you fill them in.</strong> The
        club wants them for membership — an England Athletics registration needs a date of birth
        and an address, and club kit has to be posted somewhere. You can add or change any of
        them at <a href="/account/details/">your account details</a>.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>What your account records without you typing it</h3>

    <dl class="privacy-pairs">
      <dt>When your account was created, and when it was last changed</dt>
      <dd>
        So the club can answer questions about its own records, and see whether an account
        is one somebody actually finished setting up.
      </dd>

      <dt>When you confirmed your email address, and when you last signed in</dt>
      <dd>
        A confirmation is what proves the address is yours. The last sign-in is how an
        account nobody uses can be told from one somebody does.
      </dd>

      <dt>How you sign in</dt>
      <dd>
        There is one way in today — an email address and a password — and the way you used
        is recorded with your account.
      </dd>

      <dt>Your sign-ins themselves</dt>
      <dd>
        Each time you sign in, a session is opened and our accounts provider records it,
        including the browser it was opened from and the internet address (IP address) it
        came from. It is what lets us — and you — see that somebody signed in, and end a
        session that should not be open.
      </dd>

      <dt>A cookie, to keep you signed in</dt>
      <dd>
        It holds your session and nothing else, and there is one alongside it that
        protects the forms against being submitted from another website. <strong
          >There are no advertising cookies and no analytics cookies on this website.</strong
        >
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>What the club records about what you may do</h3>

    <dl class="privacy-pairs">
      <dt>Which roles you hold</dt>
      <dd>
        Everybody with an account is a member of the website. Some people also hold a role
        that lets them read the race entries, or grant roles to others. We record which
        roles you hold, who gave you each one, when, and when it was taken away.
      </dd>

      <dt>A record of every role given or taken away</dt>
      <dd>
        Who did it, to whom, and when. It is how the club can say who was able to see
        other people's details at any point in time, so <strong
          >it is kept even if the account it is about is deleted</strong
        >.
      </dd>

      <dt>An email address the club has set a role aside for</dt>
      <dd>
        The committee can decide that a volunteer should hold a role before that person
        has signed up. Where it has, their email address is held until they do — and if
        they never sign up, it is still held until somebody removes it.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>4. What we are allowed to do with it, and why that is lawful</h2>

    <dl class="privacy-pairs">
      <dt>Give you an account, and sign you in</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Our legitimate interests.</strong> A club that cannot tell who somebody is cannot
        let them see anything of their own, and the alternative — the club's volunteers handing
        out shared passwords — is worse for everybody's information, including yours.
      </dd>

      <dt>Send you the emails your account needs</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Our legitimate interests.</strong> Confirming an address and resetting a password
        are what stop somebody else opening an account in your name, or taking over yours.
      </dd>

      <dt>Check that a form was filled in by a person</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Our legitimate interests</strong> in keeping the club's systems working. Every
        account form carries a check against automated sign-ups — see section 5.
      </dd>

      <dt>Hold the details you chose to add</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Your consent.</strong> Gender, date of birth and address are optional, you give
        them or you do not, and you can remove them again.
      </dd>

      <dt>Record who holds which role, and who granted it</dt>
      <dd>
        <strong>Our legitimate interests</strong> in knowing who could see other people's details,
        and when. A club that cannot answer that cannot look after anybody's information properly.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      We do not use your account for marketing, we do not profile you, and nothing about
      you is decided automatically in a way that affects you.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Who else sees it</h2>

    <p>
      We do not sell your information and we do not share it for marketing. Besides the
      club's own volunteers, it is handled by two companies, and both are handling it on
      the club's instructions rather than for themselves:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Supabase</strong>, who host our database and run our accounts. Your email
        address, your scrambled password, your sign-in records and everything in section 3
        are stored there, in London (AWS <code>eu-west-2</code>). The emails your account
        needs are sent by them too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong>, who serve every page of this website, and whose
        Turnstile check is what tells a person filling in an account form from an
        automated one. Serving a page means seeing the internet address (IP address) it
        was requested from; the Turnstile check sees that too, along with information
        about your browser.
      </li>
    </ul>

    {
      /* **Two processors the club does not yet use are deliberately not named here.** The
      confirmation-email service and signing in with Google are both designed and neither is
      built, and naming a company that receives nothing today is a false statement about what
      the club does — over-claiming rather than under-listing, but a false statement either
      way. `privacy.spec.ts` asserts that neither appears, exactly as `nn-privacy.spec.ts`
      already does for the entry notice, so the day one of them is wired up the test fails
      and this page is updated in the same change. */
    }
    <p>
      <strong>If the club starts using another company, this page changes with it</strong> —
      in the same change that starts using it, rather than afterwards.
    </p>

    <p>
      Your payment for a race is a separate matter, and it is Stripe's rather than ours:
      see <a href="/nn/privacy/">the {race.name} privacy notice</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>6. How long we keep it</h2>

    <dl class="privacy-pairs">
      {
        /* **The `id` is what the acceptance test holds this by**, rather than the text of
        the term beside it: the marker and the term it answers are two elements, and
        whether there is a space between them in the rendered output is Astro's whitespace
        handling rather than anything this page decides. It is also a real anchor for
        somebody sending the committee a link to the question they have to answer. */
      }
      <dt>Your account, and the details on it</dt>
      <dd id="how-long-an-account-is-kept">{orTbc(privacy.accountRetention)}</dd>

      <dt>Your sign-in records</dt>
      <dd>
        A session ends when you sign out, and expires on its own if you do not. The record
        that a sign-in happened is kept with your account.
      </dd>

      <dt>The roles you have held</dt>
      <dd>
        Kept for as long as your account is. A role that has been taken away is recorded
        as having been taken away rather than erased, so the club can still say who could
        see what, and when.
      </dd>

      <dt>The record of roles given and taken away</dt>
      <dd>
        Kept even after an account is deleted — it is the club's record of who could reach
        other people's details, and deleting it along with the account would empty that
        record at the one moment it is most likely to be asked for.
      </dd>
    </dl>

    <p>
      When a retention period ends we delete the information, or strip out anything that
      identifies you so that what is left is no longer about you.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Your rights</h2>

    <p>You can ask us to:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>give you a copy of what we hold about you;</li>
      <li>correct anything that is wrong;</li>
      <li>delete what we hold, where we no longer need it;</li>
      <li>stop or limit what we do with it;</li>
      <li>send it to you, or to someone else, in a portable form.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Asking us to delete your account is how your details are removed.</strong> You
      do not have to give a reason. Where we rely on your consent — the optional details in
      section 3 — you can withdraw it at any time, and we will act on that.
    </p>

    <p id="deleting-an-account-and-a-race-entry">
      <strong>If you have also entered a race:</strong>{' '}
      {orTbc(privacy.accountDeletionAndEntries)}
    </p>

    <p>
      Write to us using the contact in section 1. We may ask you to confirm who you are
      before we hand over or delete personal information, which is a protection for you
      rather than an obstacle.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Changes to this notice</h2>

    <p>
      If we change how we use your information we will update this page, and we will tell
      you directly if the change is one you would want to know about.
    </p>

    <p>Last updated: {orTbc(privacy.lastUpdated)}</p>
  </div>

  <footer>
    <p>
      Southville Running Club. Anything above reading "to be confirmed by the club" is
      with the committee, and this page is updated when it is settled.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/nn/privacy/">The {race.name} privacy notice</a>
    </p>
  </footer>
</Base>
